sections.s2_technical:
When you call `map.put(null, value)`, HashMap routes it through its internal `putVal` logic with a hash of 0, so the null key always lands in bucket index 0. Retrieval with `map.get(null)` follows the same path and finds it. Multiple distinct keys can map to null values with no restriction at all.

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

Map<String, java.math.BigDecimal> balances = new HashMap<>();
balances.put(null, new java.math.BigDecimal("1000000"));   // one null KEY allowed
balances.put("SAV2001", null);                             // null VALUE allowed
balances.put("CUR3001", null);                             // another null VALUE
balances.put(null, new java.math.BigDecimal("250000"));    // overwrites the null-key entry
// balances.get(null) now returns 250000
```

The senior-level nuance is that `Hashtable` and `ConcurrentHashMap` do NOT allow null keys or values and throw `NullPointerException`, which is why banking caches that must tolerate a "default/unknown account" key stay on HashMap or use a sentinel object instead.

