sections.s2_technical:
The Red-Black tree guarantees the height stays close to log n, so there is no degenerate O(n) case like an unbalanced tree.

```java
import java.util.TreeSet;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

// natural ordering via Comparable, or a Comparator supplied here:
TreeSet<Account> byBalance = new TreeSet<>(Comparator.comparing(Account::getBalance));
byBalance.add(new Account("SAV2001", "Rajesh", new BigDecimal("1000000")));
byBalance.add(new Account("CUR3001", "Priya", new BigDecimal("250000")));
// iteration yields Priya (250000) first, then Rajesh (1000000)
```

The senior nuance: TreeSet uses `compareTo`/`compare` (not `equals`) to decide uniqueness, so two elements that compare equal are treated as duplicates even if `equals` says otherwise. This matters when sorting purely by balance.

