sections.s2_technical:
A raw list forces casts and fails at runtime; a parameterised list is checked at compile time and needs no cast.

```java
import java.util.*;

List raw = new ArrayList();            // raw type, unsafe
raw.add("SAV2001");
Integer bad = (Integer) raw.get(0);    // compiles, ClassCastException at RUNTIME

List<String> accounts = new ArrayList<>();
accounts.add("SAV2001");
String id = accounts.get(0);           // no cast needed, checked at COMPILE time
// accounts.add(42);                    // compile ERROR, caught early
```

The senior point: generics are erased at runtime, so they are purely a compile-time guarantee, but that guarantee is exactly what keeps a banking codebase free of silent type-mixing bugs.

