// ═══ BlockingDeque: blocking at both ends ═══
LinkedBlockingDeque<String> deque = new LinkedBlockingDeque<>(100);

// Producer adds to tail:
deque.putLast("TXN-001");   // blocks if full (capacity 100)
// Consumer takes from head:
String txn = deque.takeFirst(); // blocks if empty

// Work-stealing: worker takes own head, thief steals from other's tail
deque.pollFirst();   // worker takes from OWN head (non-blocking)
deque.pollLast();    // thief steals from OTHER's tail (non-blocking)

// ═══ WORK-STEALING PATTERN ═══
// Thread-1 deque: [A][B][C][D][E]
//                  ↑ takeFirst      takeLast ↑
//                  worker           thief (Thread-2)
// Thread-1 processes A (head), Thread-2 steals E (tail)
// Minimal contention — they operate at opposite ends!

// ═══ ForkJoinPool uses this internally ═══
// Each ForkJoinPool worker thread has a work-stealing deque
// When thread finishes its tasks, it steals from other threads
// This keeps all CPU cores busy — excellent load balancing!
