// ═══ WHY POWER OF TWO? ═══
// capacity = 16 → capacity-1 = 15 = 0b00001111
// hash & 15 → extracts bottom 4 bits → bucket index 0-15
// This is equivalent to hash % 16 but uses bitwise AND (one CPU cycle!)
// Modulo (%) requires division — ~10x slower on most CPUs.

// ═══ RESIZE: 16 → 32 ═══
// Old: capacity=16, mask=0b00001111 (15)
// New: capacity=32, mask=0b00011111 (31)
// The mask gained ONE new bit (bit 4).

// For each entry: check if bit 4 of hash is 0 or 1:
// hash & oldCapacity (16 = 0b00010000):
//   If 0 → entry STAYS at oldIndex
//   If 1 → entry MOVES to oldIndex + 16

// Example:
// hash = 0b...0101_0011 → oldIndex = 0011 = 3, bit4 = 0 → STAYS at 3
// hash = 0b...0101_1011 → oldIndex = 1011 = 11, bit4 = 1 → MOVES to 11+16 = 27

// ═══ JAVA 8 OPTIMIZATION ═══
// Before Java 8: every entry rehashed with new mask → expensive
// Java 8+: just check ONE BIT (hash & oldCapacity) → O(1) per entry!
// Entries in each bucket split into two chains:
//   lo chain (bit=0) → stays at oldIndex
//   hi chain (bit=1) → moves to oldIndex + oldCapacity

// ═══ RESIZE TRIGGER ═══
// threshold = capacity × loadFactor = 16 × 0.75 = 12
// When size > 12 → resize!
// New capacity = 32, new threshold = 32 × 0.75 = 24
// Next resize at size > 24

// ═══ GROWTH SEQUENCE ═══
// Capacity:  16 → 32 → 64 → 128 → 256 → 512 → 1024 ...
// Threshold: 12 → 24 → 48 → 96  → 192 → 384 → 768  ...
